  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale:\n\nThe central idea is to find a function h(x) on [0,2] with integral 1 that minimizes the maximum overlapping integral with its shifted version. The current implementation uses gradient-based optimization and coordinate descent, but these may get stuck in local minima due to the problem's high dimensionality and nonconvexity. We need a population-based strategy that can escape local optima and explore solution space more effectively.\n\nThe bottleneck is that the current objective function is expensive to compute due to the full correlation calculation. While we can trade off between the objective's accuracy and noise, different algorithms can be used for exploration vs. exploitation.\n\nKey mathematically-backed facts:\n- The problem is a minimax covering problem with unit measure constraint\n- The function must be piecewise constant (in practice) for efficiency\n- The optimal function is expected to be periodic or symmetric\n- The minimal C is related to the Johnson-Lindenstrauss constant\n\nWe will use a multi-stage approach:\n- Stochastic cheaper approximations to gradient information\n- Two-population differential evolution with tournament selection\n- Local refinement with coordinate descent/sparsification\n- Multi-resolution strategy (parents/samples)\n\n2. Mathematical specification:\n\nVariables: Discrete function h \u2208 [0,1]^(N) with sum(h)=N/2\nObjective: C5 = max\u2083 GetTickCount64\u03b4\u2321 int h(x)(1-h(x+\u03b4))dx\nWith \u03b4 k from -(N-1) to N-1 in discrete samples\n\nImplementation via:\n- Fast approximate gradient estimation with control noise\n- Population evolution: maintains a community of candidate functions\n- Stochastic selection: based on expected payoff from current min/max\n- Multi-stage: 3 stages: exploration -> exploitation -> refinement\n\nSolver-based steps:\n- Type: Stochastic Differential Evolution\n- Decision vector: binary/ continuous/ hybrid representation\n- Temperature control for acceptance of worse states\n- Correlation cutoff time: run objective only for a limited range of \u03b4\n\n3. Implementation procedure:\n\nStage 0: Setup\n   - Load parent solution if available\n   - Start with grid sizes: [640, 2048] (scale with resources)\n   - Define container for best solutions: {'h': array, 'c5': float}\n\nStage 1: Exploration (300s):\n   - Represent h as piecewise constant with control points\n   - Maintain N_pop candidates with varying resolutions\n   - Evaluate objective approximate correlation using LibSVM perhaps\n   - Selection: tournament with elitism, acceptance by Gaussian noise\n\nStage 2: Exploitation (600s):\n   - Convert best candidates to continuous domain\n   - Use Nelder-Mead simplex optimizer to refine\n   - Soft projection to [0,1] space\n   - Keep track of multiple best solutions\n\nStage 3: Refinement (500s):\n   - Use coordinate descent on best solution\n   - Sparsify function by force-integral constraint\n   - Add random kicks to avoid stagnation\n   - Periodic repair of infeasibilities\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations:\n\n   Initializations:\n   -Piecewise constant from samples of [0,1] space\n   -Thinned parent solution with random noise\n   -Rejection sampling from independent blocks\n\n   Representation changes:\n   -Between domains: binary \u2192 continuous \u2192 discrete via \n   $\\text{MOE}(\\text{penalty}}$\n\n   Objective control:\n   - Shorten correlation window for speed if no signs of deception\n   - Avoid small lags which carry most weight\n   - Adaptive temperature schedule for stochastic phase\n\n   Year's sample set:\n\n   Set A: Population-based evolution with 5 populations each size 64:\n      Grid sizes: [128, 512]\n      Mutation rates: 0.1-0.5 adaptive (too high too noisy, too low too slow)\n      Selection: tournament + temperature adjustment\n\n   Set B: Two-phase solver:\n       Coarse-grid explorers (population based) upsample to high fidelity\n\n   Set C: Fine-grained sparser solution that can be interpolated\n\n   5. Budget and robustness:\n\n   Time estimates for different stages:\n\n   - Population-based evolution: Most time consuming ~400s for 128 individuals\n   - Coordinate descent: ~75s per run (parallelized)\n   - Objective computation: 20ms per evaluation at low resolution\n\n   Feasibility checks: Automatic repair via function agnostic constraint\n   Right fallback: Binary random solution that respects integral constraint\n\n   6. Validation and return contract:\n\n   - Pre-evaluate final candidate with full objective\n   - Check: sum(h) == n_points/2 and 0<=h[i]<=1 for all i\n   - Return only if c5 < threshold\n\nSummary of implementation plan:\n\nData Structures:\n- Population of dicts: {h, c5, n_bits, grid_size}\n- Soft-argmin based optimization to limit objective calls\n\nPseudocode outline:\n\nInitialize with parent\n\npossibilities = [\n    (population_search, \ncrushed_place_n=640),\n    (coarse_parallel_search,\n n=256),\n    (scripted_evolution,\n n=1024),\n    (randomized_kick_refinement,\n None),\n]\n\nfor method in possibilities:\n    if time < budget_s/4:\n        execute with params\n        take best candidate\n\nthen coordinate descent refinement as backup\n\nif no parent:\n    try_set_initial()",
